{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yang.preludes.md | sed -n '1,218p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t---\n     2\tdescription: Configurable and composable preludes for Yang language frontends — decomposing shared Layer-2a runtime building blocks (yrt.*) and decoupling frontend lowering from language runtimes via portable semantics descriptors.\n     3\t---\n     4\t\n     5\t# yang.preludes: configurable and composable preludes for Yang frontends\n     6\t\n     7\t**Status:** Proposed design, 2026-11-20. No implementation or completed acceptance gate is claimed.\n     8\t\n     9\t**Revision history:**\n    10\t- **2026-11-20 (r1 → r2 reconciliation):** Addressed all 10 findings (6 HIGH, 4 MEDIUM) from Lead System Architect review [`collab/yang-base/1795169993265-architect-yang-preludes-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`](../../collab/yang-base/1795169993265-architect-yang-preludes-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md).\n    11\t- **2026-11-21 (r2 → r3 reconciliation):** Addressed all 8 remaining findings (`F1`–`F8`: 5 HIGH, 2 MEDIUM, 1 LOW) from Lead System Architect r2 review [`collab/yang-base/1795186644731-architect-yang-preludes-design-r2.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`](../../collab/yang-base/1795186644731-architect-yang-preludes-design-r2.gpt-6-astra.findings.md).\n    12\t\n    13\t**Authority:** Subordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md) (axioms, invariants, streams, host boundaries), [`yang.antlr.md`](./yang.antlr.md) (§3 frontend SPI, §8.1–§8.2 semantic placement, §8.5.5 float addresses, §8.5.6 linked modules, §8.10 shared calling convention, §8.11 cells, §9 four-layer stdlib model), [`yang.typescript.md`](./yang.typescript.md) (§2.2 shared rulings, §17 lift enumeration, §19 owner decision A12.5), [`yang.php.md`](./yang.php.md) (§9 prelude inventory), [`yin.vm.linker.md`](./yin.vm.linker.md), [`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md), [`dao.jing.cbor.md`](./dao.jing.cbor.md), and [`src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md`](../../src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md).\n    14\t\n    15\t**Scope and amendments:** This document settles owner decision A12.5 of `yang.typescript.md` §19 (the deferred `yrt` shared prelude lift) and extends `yang.antlr.md` §3.1/§3.4/§9.3 to treat preludes as **composable runtime modules** and **swappable frontend libraries** (enabling, for example, `yang.python` syntax compiled against a `clojure.core` semantics prelude). It adds no UAST node type, no VM opcode, no manifest schema version, and no closed language registry.\n    16\t\n    17\t---\n    18\t\n    19\t## 1. Problem statement and repository baseline\n    20\t\n    21\t### 1.1 Current repository baseline\n    22\t\n    23\tTwo sharing mechanisms already exist in `src/cljc/yang/`:\n    24\t\n    25\t1. **Host-side emitter and admission machinery (`yang.core.*`):**\n    26\t   - `yang.core.prelude` (`src/cljc/yang/core/prelude.cljc`): pure UAST builders and symbol strippers (`define`, `strip`, `module-definitions`, `wide-app`, `module-tree`, `exports`, `free-names`, `defined-keys`, `free-name-vec`, `definitions->uast`, `balanced-definitions->uast`).\n    27\t   - `yang.core.prelude-admission` (`src/cljc/yang/core/prelude_admission.cljc`): `host-declaration`, `module-spec`, and `admit` over `yin.vm` and `yin.vm.module`.\n    28\t   - `yang.core.module` and `yang.core.publication`: shared linker module naming, free-primitive declaration, SCC bundle/alias specs, and dependency-first publication.\n    29\t   - *(Residual host helper duplication:)* the ClojureDart AOT `defquoted` macro is still duplicated across `yang.python.antlr.prelude`, `yang.typescript.antlr.prelude`, and `yang.php.antlr.quoted`.\n    30\t2. **One shared guest runtime module (`yang.core.call`):**\n    31\t   - `src/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc` emits the language-neutral calling convention (`yang.call`) using `yang.core.prelude` and `yang.core.prelude-admission`, with both a bundled tree (`uast`) and a linked module (`module-uast`, `module-spec`). All four frontends (`py`, `js`, `php`, `clj`) compose against it.\n    32\t\n    33\tDespite that shared plumbing, all remaining guest runtime code lives in four separate language preludes and three safepoint hook preludes:\n    34\t\n    35\t| Prelude namespace | Linker module | Size / definitions | Lowering coupling |\n    36\t|---|---|---|---|\n    37\t| `yang.python.antlr.prelude` | `py` | 4,188 LOC / 343 defs | `yang.python.antlr.lower` hardcodes 103 `py/*` names + `{:py/str}`, `{:py/float}`, `:py/None`, `:py/unbound` |\n    38\t| `yang.typescript.antlr.prelude` | `js` | 4,447 LOC / 475 defs | `yang.typescript.antlr.lower` hardcodes 93 `js/*` names + `:js/undefined`, `:js/null`, `:js/tdz`, `{:js/str}` |\n    39\t| `yang.php.antlr.prelude` (+ `_module`, `_p9`, `builtins`) | `php` | 8,150 LOC / 936 defs | `yang.php.antlr.lower` hardcodes 188 `php/*` names + `:php/null`, `{:php/type :array}`, etc. |\n    40\t| `yang.clojure.prelude` (+ inline family in `yang.clojure`) | `clj` | 250 LOC / 17 defs (+ `library`) | `yang.clojure` emits `clj/*` continuations and `yang.call/*` directly |\n    41\t| `yang.*.antlr.safepoint` | `pysp`, `jssp`, `php.sp` | 478 / 235 / 70 LOC | Hardcoded hook profiles |\n    42\t\n    43\t### 1.2 Two problems to solve together\n    44\t\n    45\t1. **Duplicated guest runtime mechanics:** ~1,200–2,000 lines of guest UAST across `py`, `js`, `php`, and `clj` implement the same core algorithms under language-prefixed names: `%capture` handler stacks, IEEE-754 float64 math (`fmod-pos` Sterbenz loop, integer-exponent squaring with split-half reciprocal `neg-power`, `nan`/`inf`/`-0.0`, `float-floor` binary descent, `float-parts`, decimal layout over `integer/float-digits`), fixed-width integer wrapping and radix digit scanning, `gen-switch` coroutine stepping, and `stream/poll!` signal safepoint hooks.\n    46\t2. **Hardcoded syntax-to-prelude coupling:** A frontend lowering currently assumes one fixed runtime profile. Even though `yang.frontend` (`src/cljc/yang/frontend.cljc`) already tracks `:yang.frontend/grammar`, `:yang.frontend/lowering-profile`, `:yang.frontend/runtime-profile`, and `:yang.frontend/support-profile` independently in its manifest, a lowering cannot today treat its target prelude as a configurable library—such as lowering Python syntax onto `clojure.core` and persistent Yin values.\n    47\t\n    48\tSeparating these two concerns while unifying their manifest model allows both **shared guest runtime reuse** (`yrt.*`) and **pluggable semantics preludes** without touching the VM kernel or shifting default language goldens during decoupling.\n    49\t\n    50\t---\n    51\t\n    52\t## 2. Derivation from foundations and non-negotiable invariants\n    53\t\n    54\tThis design is constrained directly by `docs/design/datom.world.md` and `src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md`:\n    55\t\n    56\t### 2.1 Foundational axioms\n    57\t\n    58\t1. **Everything is a Stream:** Prelude selection, composition, and lowering are pure functions of explicit data configurations and CST streams. Any external effect a prelude needs (`stream/put!`, `stream/poll!`, host I/O) remains a declared Layer-4 effect in the module manifest; prelude composition adds no ambient I/O.\n    59\t2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics:** A CST packet carries syntax only; its runtime meaning emerges from the tuple `(cst-packet, lowering-profile, semantics-descriptor, runtime-profile)`. Interpreting one Python CST under `:yang.python/py-v1` (CPython semantics) versus `:yang.python/clojure-core-v1` (Clojure persistent-data semantics) is a direct realization of \"one truth, many perspectives.\"\n    60\t3. **Code and State are Datoms (and Unitarity / Derive-Don't-Persist):**\n    61\t   - Lowering preserves source CST identity; compiled UAST roots under different preludes have distinct content addresses (or identical addresses when two descriptors emit identical UAST) linked by explicit provenance datoms pinning `(cst-address, lowering-profile, semantics-descriptor, runtime-profile, unit-address)`.\n    62\t   - Zero new AST tags, zero VM opcodes, and zero manifest schema changes are introduced. Linker manifests retain schema 1, and C1 `:yang/call-site` tables continue to publish beside manifests rather than inside them.\n    63\t   - Composition facts (which `yrt.*` modules a prelude requires) live in the explicit module `:requires` DAG, never as a redundant tag in UAST rows.\n    64\t4. **Everything is a Continuation:** Shared control-flow primitives (`yrt.escape`) and coroutine stepping (`yrt.gen`) remain pure guest UAST over `%capture` and task-heap cells (`cell/*`).\n    65\t\n    66\t### 2.2 Non-negotiable invariants\n    67\t\n    68\t1. **No hidden global state — stateless `yrt.*` modules:**\n    69\t   - `yang.core.*` holds no mutable global registry of preludes or languages (`yang.frontend` immutable catalog rule).\n    70\t   - **Shared `yrt.*` modules own zero `*.rt/*` state cells.** Every stateful `yrt.*` function takes the caller's task-local state cell (e.g., `ctx-cell`) as an explicit parameter. When a single Yin task links `py`, `js`, `php`, and `clj` modules together via `yang.call`, each language maintains its own isolated handler stack and depth/base accounting inside its own `*.rt/ctx` cell rather than aliasing or corrupting another language's stack.\n    71\t2. **No implicit control flow:** All escapes remain explicit continuation invocations over an explicit handler chain.\n    72\t3. **No raw callbacks:** Policy hooks passed by a language prelude into `yrt.*` (`on-empty`, `on-range`, `on-underflow`, `on-bit-limit`, `on-digit-limit`, `on-float-overflow`, `fin`) are ordinary guest Yin closures, never host-boundary callbacks.\n    73\t4. **No shared mutable state:** Each language profile allocates its own task-local cells once per task in its own `init!` under its own private namespace (`py.rt/*`, `js.rt/*`, `php.rt/*`, `clj.rt/*`, `clj.py.rt/*`, `clojure.core.rt/*`).\n    74\t5. **No layer collapsing:** Parsing (host ANTLR boundary) → lowering (syntax + frontend lowering contract + semantics descriptor → UAST) → prelude composition & admission (pure spec/artifact verification; never publishes or executes) → publication/linking → VM execution stay separated across explicit stream/module boundaries.\n    75\t6. **No assumed graphs:** Every composed prelude declares an explicit, acyclic `:requires` DAG over an **immutable, verified artifact closure**. Missing exports, missing transitive artifacts, or cycles fail closed at validation/admission time before publication or execution.\n    76\t7. **No closed language registry (`yang.antlr.md` owner directive):** Neither `yrt.*` nor `yang.core.*` contains a `case`/dispatch over `:python`, `:typescript`, `:php`, or `:clojure`, inspects `:py/type`/`:js/type`/`:php/type`/`:clj/type` tags, or encodes language-specific lowering ABI tables.\n    77\t8. **Security boundary — VM provenance doors stay private:** Under `src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md` and `src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc`, `cell/dsg__new-provenance` and `cell/dsg__provenance` are reserved compiler/runtime-module doors whose names guest code must not forge or re-export.\n    78\t   - **`yrt.*` modules MUST NEVER wrap, mint, read, declare, or export provenance-door primitives or `:cell/new-provenance` / `:cell/provenance` effects.**\n    79\t   - **No descriptor hook or source-facing ambient projection** may expose `cell/dsg__*`, `dsg__*`, `yin/def`, `*.rt/*`, `*.b/*`, or `yang.safepoint/*` (`§6.3`).\n    80\t   - Retained protocol-condition provenance (`yang.antlr.md` §8.10.7) stays strictly inside each trusted Layer-2b runtime module's private exception boundary.\n    81\t\n    82\t---\n    83\t\n    84\t## 3. Three-layer prelude architecture\n    85\t\n    86\t`yang.antlr.md` §9.1 Layer 2 (\"Language intrinsics and preludes\") is decomposed into three explicit sub-layers, all expressed as ordinary Universal AST over existing host modules (`cell`, `data`, `integer`, `stream`, `kind`, `portable`):\n    87\t\n    88\t```text\n    89\t+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    90\t| Layer 2c: Ambient & Standard Libraries (e.g., clojure.core, py.builtins)    |\n    91\t|   Ordinary compiled linker modules; explicit projections enter source scope |\n    92\t+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    93\t                                      ^\n    94\t                                      | binds / lowers through\n    95\t+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    96\t| Layer 2b: Semantics-Protocol Libraries (e.g., py, js, php, clj, clj.py)     |\n    97\t|   Language value encodings, coercions, error types, state cells, init!      |\n    98\t+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    99\t                                      ^\n   100\t                                      | composes explicitly via :requires DAG\n   101\t+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   102\t| Layer 2a: Composable Runtime Building Blocks (yang.call, yrt.*)             |\n   103\t|   Stateless, language-neutral UAST: yang.call, yrt.escape, yrt.float,       |\n   104\t|   yrt.int, yrt.gen, yrt.safepoint                                           |\n   105\t+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   106\t                                      ^\n   107\t                                      | host-declared pure/effect primitives\n   108\t+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   109\t| Host Registry Modules: cell | data | integer | stream | (kind, portable)    |\n   110\t+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   111\t```\n   112\t\n   113\t### 3.1 Explicit exclusions from Layer 2a (`yrt.*`)\n   114\t\n   115\tNot every similar-looking mechanism belongs in `yrt.*`. The following remain in Layer 2b (per-language preludes) because unifying them either breaks semantic fidelity or forces a closed type-tag dispatch:\n   116\t\n   117\t| Mechanism | Why it stays in Layer 2b |\n   118\t|---|---|\n   119\t| **Ordered maps / dicts / arrays** | Python dicts normalize `1 == 1.0 == True` via exact rationals and tuple-key recursion; JS own-key maps sort uint32 indices ahead of strings and symbols; PHP arrays coerce int64-looking strings, track `:next-index`, and implement copy-on-write refcounted handles. Only the core storage idioms (`yin.vm.data`) are shared. |\n   120\t| **Module / import loaders** | Python `sys.modules` + package hierarchy, JS ESM link/eval stacks + live bindings, and PHP dynamic `include`/`require` scope-cell adoption have incompatible lifecycle rules. |\n   121\t| **Object / class / prototype dispatch** | MRO/descriptors (`py`), prototype chains/accessors (`js`), and traits/refcounted destructors (`php`) belong to `yang.antlr.md` §9.4 Layer 3. |\n   122\t| **Provenance doors** | `cell/dsg__new-provenance` and `cell/dsg__provenance` are security-sensitive VM doors (`§2.2` invariant 8). |\n   123\t| **PHP `php.sp` budget/ticks hooks** | `php.sp` uses a decrementing execution-budget cell (`php.sp/remaining`) and `:line :after` statement ticks rather than `stream/poll!` signal cursors. |\n   124\t\n   125\t---\n   126\t\n   127\t## 4. Layer 2a: composable `yrt.*` module specifications\n   128\t\n   129\tEvery Layer-2a module follows the single-source dual-emitter pattern already established by `yang.core.call`: one ordered definition vector emitting both a bundled `uast` (tail-marked) and a linked `module-uast` + `module-spec` (wide-app over stripped definitions, C1-finalized call sites, and `prelude-admission/module-spec`).\n   130\t\n   131\t### 4.1 `yang.call` *(existing)*\n   132\t\n   133\tRemains unchanged at `src/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc` (`yang.antlr.md` §8.10): structural callable discovery (`entry`, `eligible?`), invocation records (`invocation`, `invocation-with`, `valid-invocation?`), and condition transfer (`condition`, `thrown`, `transfer`, `invoke`, `invoke-with`). Requires no `yrt.*` module so `yang.call` stays a leaf dependency.\n   134\t\n   135\t### 4.2 `yrt.escape` — `%capture` handler stack and escapes\n   136\t\n   137\tUsed by: `py`, `js`, `php`, `clj`, `clj.py`. Host dependencies: `cell/get`, `cell/set!`, `cell/new`, `data/nth`.\n   138\t\n   139\tReconciles the current per-language divergences (`4-slot` vs `5-slot` frames, `:base` rebasing, `:generator` boundary frames, `orelse` vs value-return, PHP `abandon-to`, normal-exit `finally` sentinel, and empty-stack handling) by parameterizing `ctx-cell`, `normal-arg`, and `on-empty`:\n   140\t\n   141\t- **Unified context record:** `{:handlers hs :depth d :base b :frame f}`.\n   142\t  - **4-to-5-slot migration for `js` and `clj`:** Currently `yang.typescript.antlr.prelude` and `yang.clojure.prelude` allocate `{:handlers hs}` and push 4-slot frames `[tag payload rest n]`. In P1, `js/init!`, `clj/init!`, and bundled `yang.clojure` migrate to `(yrt.escape/ctx nil 0 0 nil)` and 5-slot frames.\n   143\t  - **Distinct slot meanings (`n` vs. `depth`):** In the 5-slot frame `[kind payload rest n depth]`:\n   144\t    - `kind`: `:finally`, `:handler`, `:generator`, or any language-installed non-`:finally` boundary tag.\n   145\t    - `payload`: continuation `r` (for `:handler` / `:generator` boundary dispatch) or cleanup closure `fin` (for `:finally`).\n   146\t    - `rest`: enclosing frame (or `nil`).\n   147\t    - **`n` (slot 3):** monotonic handler-stack frame count `(+ 1 (frame-depth rest))` used strictly by `unwind-to` and `abandon-to` for O(1) structural depth comparison (`<= (data/nth hs 3) n`).\n   148\t    - **`depth` (slot 4):** activation-relative function call depth at the moment the frame was pushed, restored onto `:depth` by `pop-frame` (`py` and `php` recursion/safepoint accounting). Languages without call-depth hooks (`js`, `clj`) keep `depth = 0`.\n   149\t- **Capture-safe vector frame reads (`data/nth`):** Every read of the 5-slot frame vector (`kind`, `payload`, `rest`, `n`, `depth`) inside `yrt.escape` uses **`data/nth`** (`(data/nth hs 0)` … `(data/nth hs 4)`), reserving `get` strictly for the context-map fields (`:handlers`, `:depth`, `:base`). Because `data/nth` is a qualified host module export, source-defined `(defn get ...)` can never corrupt frame dispatch or stack traversal.\n   150\t- **Open boundary-frame dispatch (preserving generators in P1):**\n   151\t  - Both `py/gen-start` (`yang.python.antlr.prelude:471`) and `php/gen-start` (`yang.php.antlr.prelude:5312`) install `:generator` frames whose payload is `(fn [e] (gen-fail g e))`.\n   152\t  - Accordingly, `yrt.escape/raise` MUST dispatch `(if (= (data/nth hs 0) :finally) ... ((data/nth hs 1) e))` — treating `:finally` specially and delivering `e` to `(data/nth hs 1)` for **every non-`:finally` frame kind** (`:handler`, `:generator`, etc.), never restricting allowed kinds to a closed two-kind check! Existing generators remain 100% compatible during P1 even before `yrt.gen` is extracted in P4.\n   153\t- **`restore!` invariant:** `(fn [ctx-cell saved] (cell/set! ctx-cell (assoc saved :base (get (cell/get ctx-cell) :base))))` — always preserves the activation's current `:base` across generator crossings.\n   154\t\n   155\t**Public exports:**\n   156\t\n   157\t| Export | Signature | Behavior |\n   158\t|---|---|---|\n   159\t| `ctx` | `[handlers depth base frame]` | Construct context record |\n   160\t| `handlers` / `set-handlers!` / `restore!` | `[ctx-cell ...]` | Context cell accessors/mutators (`restore!` preserves live `:base`) |\n   161\t| `frame-depth` / `frame` / `pop-frame` | `[hs]` / `[ctx-cell kind payload rest]` / `[ctx-cell hs]` | Frame construction and unwind pop (restoring `:handlers` and `:depth` via `data/nth`) |\n   162\t| `raise` | `[ctx-cell e on-empty]` | Pop & invoke `:finally` frames passing `e`; invoke `(data/nth hs 1)` on any non-`:finally` frame; call `(on-empty e)` if stack is empty (`py`/`js`/`clj` return a sentinel; `php` triggers `invariant-violation`) |\n   163\t| `unwind-to` | `[ctx-cell n normal-arg]` | Pop frames while `(> (data/nth hs 3) n)`, running each `:finally` thunk with `normal-arg` (`:py/None`, `:js/undefined`, `:php/null`, `nil`) |\n   164\t| `abandon-to` | `[ctx-cell n]` | Pop frames while `(> (data/nth hs 3) n)` **without** running `:finally` thunks (PHP terminal fatals / `exit`) |\n   165\t| `try-orelse` | `[ctx-cell body handler orelse]` | Install `:handler`; on normal exit restore and tail-call `(orelse)`; on escape restore and call `(handler r)` (`py`, `php`, and Python `try/except/else` in `clj.py`) |\n   166\t| `try-value` | `[ctx-cell body handler]` | Install `:handler`; on normal exit restore and return `(body)`'s value directly (`js`, `clj`, and `clj.py` / `clojure.core` function entry boundaries) |\n   167\t| `try-finally` | `[ctx-cell body fin normal-arg]` | Install `:finally`; on normal exit restore, run `(fin normal-arg)`, return `v` |\n   168\t| `call-ec` | `[ctx-cell f normal-arg]` | Capture escape continuation that runs `unwind-to` then `restore!` before invoking `r` |\n   169\t| `call-terminal` | `[ctx-cell f]` | Capture terminal escape that runs `abandon-to` then `restore!` before invoking `r` (`php`) |\n   170\t\n   171\t**Capture-safety for bundled `yang.clojure`:**\n   172\t- In `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2470–2517`, the live preamble currently constructs **four** portable guest helpers (`dsg__get`, `dsg__equal`, `dsg__assoc`, `dsg__empty`), while `conj` and `vec` are protected against local shadowing by the convention's binder checks (`clojure.cljc:2479`), and `+` / `<=` currently have no `dsg__*` helpers. Note that `dsg__get` (`clojure.cljc:2484–2497`) scans only maps (`data/map?`) and returns `nil` on vectors.\n   173\t- Because `yrt.escape` accesses all vector frames via `data/nth`, bundled `yrt.escape/uast` needs `get` only for context maps (`:handlers`, `:depth`, `:base`). Even so, as defense-in-depth, extending `dsg__get` (or keeping a dedicated guest helper) to handle non-negative integer indexing on `data/vector?` via `data/nth` ensures no bundled frame/context lookup can ever return `nil` when source code defines `(defn get ...)`.\n   174\t- Thus, when `yrt.escape/uast` is bundled inline into non-linked `yang.clojure`, it reads `get`, `=`, `assoc`, `conj`, `+`, and `<=` as follows without breaking UCF machine-data validation (never binding host primitive functions inside guest environments):\n   175\t  - `[get = assoc]` bind to the existing portable guest closures `[dsg__get dsg__equal dsg__assoc]` (with `dsg__get` supporting both maps and vectors, and `yrt.escape` reading frames via `data/nth`);\n   176\t  - `conj` continues to rely on `yang.clojure`'s binder-check refusal;\n   177\t  - `+` and `<=` are wrapped by adding two portable guest closures `dsg__add` and `dsg__lte` (using `(+ a b)` / `(<= a b)` when unshadowed, and safe binary integer arithmetic or binder-check refusal when `+` or `<=` is defined in `(::defined env)` — all pure guest lambdas, matching `dsg__get` at `clojure.cljc:2484`).\n   178\t- In linked mode, module lexical isolation makes the `dsg__*` wrapper unnecessary.\n   179\t\n   180\t### 4.3 `yrt.float` — IEEE-754 float64 math seam\n   181\t\n   182\tUsed by: `py`, `js`, `php`, `clj.py`, standalone `clojure.core` (omitted by default `clj`). Host dependencies: `data/float64`, `data/float-value`, `data/float64?` (see §7.2 prerequisite), `data/content=`, `data/count`, `data/nth`, `data/str-concat`, `integer/shift-right`, `integer/bit-and`, `integer/sub`, `integer/floor-div-mod`, `integer/float-digits`, `integer/decimal->float`.\n   183\t\n   184\t**Representational, domain, and strict raw-double isolation rules:**\n   185\t1. **Boxed-boundary vs. leaf-raw exports (zero raw-double callbacks):**\n   186\t   - `yrt.float` never inspects or emits `{:py/float ...}` tags; `py` wraps/unwraps at its thin boundary (owner decision D3).\n   187\t   - **No-capture / no-safepoint / no-callback rule:** Pure `*-raw` leaf helpers act on unwrapped host doubles (`data/float-value`) strictly inside non-escaping, non-safepoint frames and **accept zero guest callback parameters** (never invoking `%capture`, safepoints, or arbitrary guest closures while an unwrapped double is live).\n   188\t   - Every cross-boundary numeric function that can encounter an exceptional edge condition (`neg-pow-result`, `floor-int-result`, `float-floor-result`) takes a **boxed `data/float64` input** and returns either a **boxed `data/float64` / exact integer** OR a **pure status keyword** (`:yrt.float/underflow`, `:yrt.float/out-of-range`) **after** exiting the leaf frame. Outer Layer-2b wrappers (`py`, `js`, `php`, `clj.py`) inspect the returned status keyword safely outside any raw-double stack frame and only then raise language-specific exceptions or compute signed `±Inf` from the boxed input and exact exponent.\n   189\t   - Preserves `yang.antlr.md` §8.5.5: zero bare integral float literals (`(data/float-value 1)` instead of `1.0`), `-0.0` constructed as `(* (data/float-value -1) (data/float-value 0))`.\n   190\t2. **Exact exponentiation rounding reconciliation (`fpow-half` vs. `fpow-base`):**\n   191\t   - Live `py/fpow` (`yang.python.antlr.prelude:1514`) and `js/fpow` (`yang.typescript.antlr.prelude:617`) recursively compute the half-power `h = fpow(base, floor(e/2))` and square `h * h` (multiplying by `base` when `e` is odd).\n   192\t   - Live `php/pow-sq` (`yang.php.antlr.prelude:1361`) squares the base first (`pow-sq(x * x, floor(e/2))`) and multiplies by `x` when `e` is odd.\n   193\t   - Those two evaluation orders round differently at the final ULP — e.g. for `1.1 ** 6`, half-power squaring yields `0x1.c585058dde7acp+0` while base squaring yields `0x1.c585058dde7abp+0`.\n   194\t   - To avoid silently breaking PHP or Python/JS oracle parity in P1, `yrt.float` exports **both** `fpow-half-raw` (selected by `:half`, for `py`/`js`/`clj.py`) and `fpow-base-raw` (selected by `:base`, for `php`) — see owner decision D7 if a future unified rounding rule is ever approved.\n   195\t\n   196\t**Public exports and domain preconditions:**\n   197\t\n   198\t| Export | Signature & Preconditions / Domain | Return contract (boxed or pure leaf) |\n   199\t|---|---|---|\n   200\t| `inf`, `neg-inf`, `nan`, `zero`, `neg-zero` | `[]` | Return `data/float64` boxed constants |\n   201\t| `nan?`, `finite?`, `zero?`, `neg-zero?`, `abs`, `neg`, `zero-like` | Unwrapped host double `x` inside leaf frames (`zero-like` takes `[y]`); no `%capture`/safepoints | Pure leaf boolean or unwrapped double (`nan?`: `(not (<= x x))`; `finite?`: `(< (abs x) (data/float-value ##Inf))`; `zero-like`: `copysign(0.0, y)`) |\n   202\t| `fdiv-raw`, `fdiv` | `fdiv-raw [x y]` (leaf raw); `fdiv [x-boxed y-boxed]` (boxed) | IEEE division preserving `y`'s signed zero on overflow to `±Inf` and `0/0 -> NaN`; `fdiv` returns `data/float64` |\n   203\t| `fmod-pos` | **Leaf raw; requires finite `x >= 0` and finite `y > 0`** (callers exclude NaN, `±Inf`, `y = 0`, negative signs beforehand) | Pure Sterbenz long-division remainder loop; returns unwrapped double inside caller leaf frame |\n   204\t| `fpow-half-raw`, `fpow-base-raw` | **Leaf raw; requires unwrapped `base`, exact integer `e >= 0`** | Integer-exponent squaring (`py`/`js` order vs. `php` order); zero callbacks |\n   205\t| `neg-pow-result` | **Boxed boundary `[x-boxed n pow-order]`**: `x-boxed` is `data/float64`, `n > 0` exact integer, `pow-order` in `#{:half :base}` | Unwraps `x-boxed` inside leaf frame, computes `1 / x^n` (splitting halves `h` and `n - h` when `x^n` overflows to `Inf`); returns boxed **`data/float64`** OR status keyword **`:yrt.float/underflow`** (never invokes callbacks while raw doubles are live) |\n   206\t| `floor-int-result`, `float-floor-result` | **Boxed boundary `[x-boxed]`**: `x-boxed` is `data/float64` | Binary descent for finite `|x| < 2^53`; returns exact integer (`floor-int-result`) / `data/float64` (`float-floor-result`) OR status keyword **`:yrt.float/out-of-range`** (for `|x| >= 2^53`, `±Inf`, `NaN`) |\n   207\t| `float-parts` | **Requires finite nonzero unwrapped `x`** (caller excludes `0.0`, `NaN`, `±Inf` beforehand) | Returns exact `[mantissa exp2]` integer pair (safe integer tuple; no raw double returned) |\n   208\t| `zeros`, `exp-text` | Exact counts/exponents | Pure string layout helpers over `integer/float-digits` |\n   209\t\n   210\t### 4.4 `yrt.int` — exact-integer helpers and fixed-width wrapping\n   211\t\n   212\tUsed by: `py`, `js`, `php`, `clj.py` (omitted by `clj`). Host dependencies: `data/count`, `data/nth`, `integer/*`.\n   213\t\n   214\t**`yin.vm.integer` v4 refusal algebra (`src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc:81–100`):**\n   215\t- `yin.vm.integer` v4 **returns** keyword reasons in place of a result for exactly **three** resource/range conditions: `:yin.vm.integer/bit-limit`, `:yin.vm.integer/digit-limit`, and `:yin.vm.integer/float-overflow`.\n   216\t- Every other refusal (`:arity`, `:wrong-type`, `:out-of-range`, `:syntax`, `:zero-division`, `:negative-count`, `:negative-exponent`) **throws** a host operational failure indicating a caller-side defect (`integer.cljc:93–100`) and **MUST NOT** be caught or relabeled into guest exceptions.\n   217\t\n   218\t**Public exports:**\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
