{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yang.antlr.md | sed -n '2529,2610p;3558,3642p;3841,3955p'\"","aggregated_output":"  2529\t#### 8.5.5 Float addresses\n  2530\t\n  2531\tThis section records the float-address ruling and the converged sign-off\n  2532\trulings that followed it. The float-fix slice implements them, as the one\n  2533\tpreparatory slice that lands before any float-bearing cross-host pin;\n  2534\t\"Implementation\" below records what it does.\n  2535\t\n  2536\tThe codec conforms; the producers are defective. On JavaScript,\n  2537\t`dao.jing.cbor` classifies an integral Number other than negative zero\n  2538\tas an integer, exactly as `dao.jing.cbor.md` (*Numeric identity*)\n  2539\trequires: JavaScript callers must use the float64 carrier for integral\n  2540\tfloats. The Python producers lose the float kind before encoding:\n  2541\t\n  2542\t- Source literals. Lowering wraps a bare host number as\n  2543\t  `{:py/float v}`. The wrapper carries Python type semantics only; Jing\n  2544\t  classifies the nested number by host, so `2.0` is integer 2 on Node\n  2545\t  and float64 on JVM and Dart.\n  2546\t- Prelude constants. The quoted prelude holds integral float literals\n  2547\t  such as `1.0`, which the ClojureScript reader collapses to `1` before\n  2548\t  any code runs.\n  2549\t- Runtime values. `py/float` builds `{:py/float (* 1.0 x)}`, so `4/2` is\n  2550\t  `{:py/float 2}` on Node, and any addressed image holding it diverges.\n  2551\t- The loud variant: an integral float at or above 2^53 hits Node's\n  2552\t  unsafe-integer refusal.\n  2553\t\n  2554\tBoth rejected alternatives fail for the same reason, that the kind is\n  2555\tgone before the codec sees the number. Normalizing integral floats to\n  2556\tintegers already describes Node; extending it to JVM and Dart violates\n  2557\t\"`1` and `1.0` have different addresses\". Tagging row inputs alone\n  2558\tleaves runtime values diverging and gives Node two representations,\n  2559\tsince a JVM-minted float row already decodes to the carrier on Node.\n  2560\t\n  2561\tThe normative rule, astra's text as widened by fable's correction 1 to\n  2562\truntime values:\n  2563\t\n  2564\t> Every floating-point value entering canonical rows or any addressed\n  2565\t> image, including source literals, prelude constants, nested Python\n  2566\t> float payloads, and runtime float values held by snapshots and\n  2567\t> continuations, must preserve float64 kind before host representation\n  2568\t> erases it. Addressed values use Jing's existing float64\n  2569\t> representation. A Python float wrapper alone does not establish the\n  2570\t> numeric kind of its payload. Row projection and transformation must\n  2571\t> preserve that kind.\n  2572\t\n  2573\tThe mechanism is Jing's existing `dao.jing/float64` carrier (tag 27),\n  2574\tinserted at the producer, under these constraints:\n  2575\t\n  2576\t- One representation. Every `:py/float` payload is Jing float64 content,\n  2577\t  `cbor/float64` of the value: the identity on JVM and Dart, the carrier\n  2578\t  on JavaScript. On JavaScript every payload is a carrier, non-integral\n  2579\t  values included, since a carrier equals only another carrier.\n  2580\t- Kind is fixed where it is still known. Lowering constructs the carrier\n  2581\t  while literal syntax still identifies a float; the prelude marks its\n  2582\t  float constants explicitly before JavaScript collapses them. A later\n  2583\t  scan of bare numbers cannot recover the distinction.\n  2584\t- The prelude seam. `py/float` wraps and `py/num` unwraps through two\n  2585\t  new pure functions in the `data` module's table (section 9.3);\n  2586\t  arithmetic between them stays host-native on bare numbers. The quoted\n  2587\t  prelude carries no integral float literal: `(* 1.0 x)` coercions and\n  2588\t  constants such as `1.0` and `0.0` go through the data functions, while\n  2589\t  a non-integral constant such as `0.5` stays. A JVM test fails on any\n  2590\t  integral-valued double in a prelude literal row.\n  2591\t- No capture inside the seam: no yield or safepoint sits between an\n  2592\t  unwrap and its rewrap. Whether the safepoint stage marks sites inside\n  2593\t  prelude bodies is unverified, so a test pins this.\n  2594\t- No new tag, payload kind, or row shape. Tag 27, signed-zero\n  2595\t  preservation, NaN normalization, and the refusals of equal-value,\n  2596\t  different-kind collection collisions are unchanged. The generic codec\n  2597\t  learns no Python map semantics; its canonical encoding contract and\n  2598\t  frozen fixtures are not edited. The converged refusal ruling amends\n  2599\t  this for the carrier's behavior only (below).\n  2600\t\n  2601\tCarrier insertion is not a drop-in runtime fix. An execution bridge\n  2602\tadmits the carrier at all three scalar gates, `plain-data?` and\n  2603\t`machine-data?` in `yin.vm` (the row and machine-payload gates, which\n  2604\totherwise refuse to build a carrier-bearing row) and `scalar?` in\n  2605\t`yin.vm.engine`, and audits the kind classifier in `yin.vm.values`.\n  2606\tThe exact-integer carrier is the precedent (section 8.5.4), and\n  2607\t`yin.vm.debruijn` already sees through the float carrier. Values keep\n  2608\ttheir float kind whenever they return to addressed data. Two boundary\n  2609\taudits follow: `float-repr` in the renderer unwraps the carrier on\n  2610\tJavaScript, and Node test expectations comparing bare numbers move to\n  3558\t#### 8.10.2 Callable representation and identity\n  3559\t\n  3560\tKeep the callable's existing identity and source-specific data. Add the\n  3561\tcommon entry beside its private code and metadata:\n  3562\t\n  3563\t```clojure\n  3564\t;; Illustrative contents of one task-owned Python function cell.\n  3565\t;; `entry` and `body-code` are live Yin closures, not literal AST maps.\n  3566\t{:yang.call/entry entry\n  3567\t :py/type :function\n  3568\t :spec parameter-spec\n  3569\t :defaults defaults\n  3570\t :code body-code}\n  3571\t```\n  3572\t\n  3573\tThe existing `:code` field is not the shared entry: its signature differs\n  3574\tbetween preludes. It may remain private. Cross-language callers must not\n  3575\tinspect it or extract it to bypass the entry.\n  3576\t\n  3577\tA cell-backed callable stores the entry in its cell contents. An\n  3578\timmutable callable descriptor, such as a bound method, may carry it\n  3579\tdirectly in its map. The common prelude projects the entry structurally:\n  3580\tread an existing sealed cell reference through `cell/get`, or inspect\n  3581\tthe immutable descriptor, then obtain `:yang.call/entry`. A\n  3582\tcell-ref-shaped map is dereferenced only when the heap authenticates it\n  3583\t(`cell/authentic?`, section 8.10.12); an unauthenticated shape projects\n  3584\tto itself and follows the ordinary descriptor rules, so data imitating\n  3585\tthe reference language stays data. The projection must not\n  3586\tdispatch on `:py/type`, `:js/type`, `:php/type`, language names, or a\n  3587\tregistry of languages.\n  3588\t\n  3589\tAn admitted callable is a runtime cell/descriptor constructed or exposed\n  3590\tby the callable constructors or import/export projections of a pinned,\n  3591\tconforming profile. The entry slot is a runtime descriptor field, not a\n  3592\tsource property that promotes arbitrary data to a function. A Python\n  3593\tdictionary, JavaScript object, or PHP array containing a source key\n  3594\tspelled `:yang.call/entry` remains ordinary source data unless an explicit\n  3595\tprofile-defined projection makes it a callable descriptor. Source\n  3596\tproperty access must not overwrite or masquerade as this runtime slot.\n  3597\tThis is an obligation of construction and source resolution, not a new\n  3598\tVM type, descriptor seal, or runtime provenance/language registry. Once\n  3599\tthe resolved descriptor is admitted, the shared projection is structural;\n  3600\tUAST deliberately constructing a conforming descriptor opts into the\n  3601\tconvention rather than changing a source language's data-map semantics.\n  3602\t\n  3603\tMissing entry means not callable. Constructors must install a genuine\n  3604\tunary Yin closure, and supported source mutations must preserve that\n  3605\tinvariant. Unary arity is a constructor/profile conformance obligation,\n  3606\tnot a signature introspection operation: Yin closures are opaque and the\n  3607\tVM's existing argument binding does not enforce this convention's arity.\n  3608\tA key in a map grants no VM privilege; application, ownership, and\n  3609\teffect checks still apply. A non-applicable entry remains an operational\n  3610\tVM application defect, not a source exception caught by the shared helper.\n  3611\t\n  3612\tFor callable kinds whose eligibility can change, the same cell or\n  3613\tdescriptor may also contain `:yang.call/eligible?`, a unary Yin predicate\n  3614\ton the original callable. Without it, an entry is eligible by default.\n  3615\tWith it, the common callable test uses its boolean result. The predicate\n  3616\treads explicit runtime metadata/heap only; it must not mutate state,\n  3617\tevaluate arguments, execute the target body, or invoke getters/user hooks.\n  3618\tIt has the same constructor/arity obligations as the entry. For example,\n  3619\tan instance can keep one dispatch entry while eligibility follows whether\n  3620\tits class currently supplies a supported call method; changing the class\n  3621\tdoes not require rewriting every instance.\n  3622\t\n  3623\tCall eligibility is tested at the callability-check point required by\n  3624\tthe caller's source, not universally before argument evaluation. The\n  3625\tentry resolves/rechecks a dynamic target at its source-required dispatch\n  3626\tpoint so argument side effects that change eligibility remain visible.\n  3627\tStatic callable kinds need no predicate.\n  3628\t\n  3629\tSource callable designators, such as PHP function-name strings and\n  3630\tcallable arrays where supported, are resolved under their source's rules\n  3631\tbefore common invocation. They do not gain entry slots merely by being\n  3632\tstrings or arrays. Exporting a designator as a first-class callable needs\n  3633\tan explicit frontend projection to an admitted descriptor, retaining any\n  3634\tsource-required late lookup and receiver semantics. Passing the raw\n  3635\tstring or array as data leaves it unchanged; a foreign caller must not\n  3636\tguess its language or search an ambient namespace to invoke it. This is\n  3637\tsource resolution/projection, not a language-pair wrapper around an\n  3638\talready admitted function.\n  3639\t\n  3640\tCross-language passage must not allocate a replacement function cell.\n  3641\tReturned functions retain their original identity, captures, defaults,\n  3642\tbound receiver, properties, and static state. A receiving frontend's\n  3841\t#### 8.10.7 Normal return and escaping conditions\n  3842\t\n  3843\tNormal return is the value of ordinary Yin application. There is no\n  3844\textra return continuation, completion packet around each result, or new\n  3845\tlanguage-specific return instruction.\n  3846\t\n  3847\tAn escaping source exception invokes the supplied raise exit with:\n  3848\t\n  3849\t```clojure\n  3850\t{:yang.call/reason :yang.call/thrown\n  3851\t :yang.call/value  original-thrown-value}\n  3852\t```\n  3853\t\n  3854\tThis is the argument to an exceptional continuation transfer, not an\n  3855\tordinary return. Returning an identical-looking map returns normal data.\n  3856\tLikewise, a source program throwing a map with these keys throws that\n  3857\tmap as the **payload of a new condition**; its shape is never evidence\n  3858\tthat it is already a condition to forward. No engine-effect value is\n  3859\tsynthesized.\n  3860\t\n  3861\tProtocol failures use qualified reasons such as\n  3862\t`:yang.call/not-callable`, `:yang.call/invalid-invocation`, and\n  3863\t`:yang.call/unsupported-binding`, with plain evidence. The caller maps\n  3864\tthem to a documented local exception form retaining the complete\n  3865\toriginal condition. Callee argument-binding failures retain the actual\n  3866\tcallee exception under `:yang.call/thrown`. These reasons do not extend\n  3867\t`dao.stream`'s outcome algebra.\n  3868\t\n  3869\tThe receiving caller interprets an escaping exception as follows:\n  3870\t\n  3871\t1. A payload valid as an exception in its own runtime retains that\n  3872\t   runtime's ordinary catching and identity behavior.\n  3873\t2. A runtime permitting arbitrary thrown values, such as JavaScript,\n  3874\t   may deliver the original payload directly.\n  3875\t3. A runtime requiring exception objects, such as Python or PHP,\n  3876\t   represents an otherwise foreign payload with its own\n  3877\t   `ForeignException`, retaining the complete condition and original\n  3878\t   payload. Catch-all catches this local object. Typed source catches\n  3879\t   match its local hierarchy, never guessed foreign class names.\n  3880\t\n  3881\t`ForeignException` is a proposed prelude-owned type, not a VM type.\n  3882\tUnchanged rethrow propagates the original condition without adding\n  3883\twrappers. Raising a new exception creates a new condition. Crossing back\n  3884\tinto a runtime recognizing the original exception restores its original\n  3885\ttype and identity. Formatting a foreign payload must not implicitly\n  3886\tinvoke a foreign method.\n  3887\t\n  3888\tThe runtime associates its generated wrapper's identity with the retained\n  3889\tcondition for the wrapper's entire lifetime, separate from source-writable\n  3890\tattributes. The association normally lives in private wrapper-cell\n  3891\tcontents, or an equivalent task-owned identity structure with the same\n  3892\tlifetime. It survives storing or returning the wrapper after the creating\n  3893\tcall/catch activation has ended, and remains recoverable when that same\n  3894\twrapper is rethrown in another activation. Temporary catch state alone is\n  3895\tinsufficient. Reachability/reclamation must retain the condition and its\n  3896\tpayload while the wrapper is live, without an ambient registry.\n  3897\t\n  3898\tThe runtime must not detect wrappers or unwrap arbitrary maps by their\n  3899\tkey names. JavaScript may expose raw payloads for\n  3900\t`:yang.call/thrown`; its local exception for a protocol failure must still\n  3901\tretain that failure's qualified condition. Rethrowing the same generated\n  3902\texception forwards that complete condition, including protocol reasons;\n  3903\tthrowing an unrelated value creates a new `:yang.call/thrown` condition.\n  3904\tSaved activation state refers to this identity-owned association; exiting\n  3905\tor restoring a catch does not destroy it.\n  3906\t\n  3907\tVM application/custody defects and host implementation failures remain\n  3908\toperational failures. Do not catch them broadly and relabel them as\n  3909\tsource exceptions. Existing explicit stream/module refusals keep their\n  3910\tsource mappings under the relevant profiles.\n  3911\t\n  3912\t#### 8.10.8 Cleanup, re-entry, and suspension\n  3913\t\n  3914\tEach activation establishes its own exception bridge and saves its\n  3915\tcallee runtime's dynamic context. Binding, body, and exit run under that\n  3916\tbridge. Before invoking the caller's raise exit, let source catches and\n  3917\tcleanup complete and restore saved handler/depth/frame state. Normal\n  3918\treturn restores it too. A source `finally` replacing a pending return\n  3919\tor exception does so before common propagation under its own rules.\n  3920\t\n  3921\tIn A-to-B-to-A calls, the innermost activation must not jump to an older\n  3922\tA handler past B. Its entry bridge is the boundary: inner A cleanup\n  3923\tprecedes B cleanup, which precedes outer A handling. Every activation\n  3924\towns its saved state and raise exit, without a single current-foreign-\n  3925\thandler cell or ambient dispatcher. Unchanged rethrow preserves the\n  3926\tinner exception as specified in section 8.10.7.\n  3927\t\n  3928\tCleanup runs once per actual exit. Resuming a suspended VM transition\n  3929\tmust not repeat parameter binding, argument evaluation, defaults,\n  3930\treference acquisition, or completed cleanup. The record, continuations,\n  3931\tand explicit runtime state use the existing continuation machinery and\n  3932\tits admission restrictions.\n  3933\t\n  3934\tA generator or asynchronous function returns its source-defined object.\n  3935\tSuspension on a stream effect keeps the activation; it is not a new\n  3936\tcall. A later generator resume or scheduled job has a new activation and\n  3937\tvalid bridge, never the raise exit of a call that already returned.\n  3938\tUnrestricted continuation escape across foreign frames is not implicitly\n  3939\tsupported: a claiming profile must define and test restoration and\n  3940\treference lifetimes for every crossed frame.\n  3941\t\n  3942\tInvoking a function-valued argument is internal Yin computation.\n  3943\tHost-initiated callbacks remain stream events under\n  3944\t`datom.world.abi.md`; this convention adds no host callback facility.\n  3945\t\n  3946\t#### 8.10.9 Publication, visibility, and reproducibility\n  3947\t\n  3948\tShared callability creates no common global scope. An import or\n  3949\tcomposition explicitly binds exports in the receiving source namespace\n  3950\tunder its normal name/module rules. Missing names keep their source\n  3951\terrors. No fallback searches other frontends, the filesystem, a host\n  3952\tpackage registry, or ambient globals.\n  3953\t\n  3954\tLinked modules publish code and immutable specifications. Their bodies\n  3955\tinstantiate function objects and common entries in the importing task,\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
