lf (get (integer/floor-div-mod n 2) 0)\nsrc/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:1367:           (if (= (get (integer/floor-div-mod n 2) 1) 1) (* r x) r))))]\nsrc/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:1375:           (let [h (get (integer/floor-div-mod n 2) 0)]\nsrc/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:1380:               (if (= (get (integer/floor-div-mod n 2) 1) 1)\nsrc/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:1410:             (if (= (get (integer/floor-div-mod y 2) 1) 0) 1 -1)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'tail -32 collab/yang-base/1795186644731-architect-yang-preludes-design-r2.gpt-6-astra.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'tail -32 collab/yang-base/1795186644731-architect-yang-preludes-design-r2.gpt-6-astra.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"\nBinding shared escape-runtime `get` directly to that helper therefore breaks frame dispatch and traversal in this supported shadowing case. This is a concrete mismatch in the proposed reuse plan.\n\nExtend the portable helper to cover the required vector semantics, or use a dedicated guest frame accessor built from `data/nth`. Test bundled/linked parity with source-defined `get` and active exception/finally frames.\n\n**F7 — MEDIUM | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:201](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:201) | Policy-hook signatures contradict the raw-double capture rule | Separate raw computation from callback invocation.**\n\nLine 184 prohibits arbitrary guest calls while unwrapped doubles remain live. Nevertheless, lines 201–202 specify raw-double `x` and invoke `(on-underflow x n)` or `(on-range x)`.\n\nCalling the location a “guard boundary” does not make that argument or the suspended environment portable.\n\nDefine the boundary concretely: raw helpers should return boxed results or safe status data, then a wrapper should invoke policy closures after leaving the raw arithmetic frame. Alternatively, provide an equally explicit capture-safe protocol. Cover exceptional hook paths in the P1 gate, not only successful arithmetic.\n\n**F8 — LOW | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:279](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:279) | The example effect ceiling rejects its own runtime | Omit the ceiling or provide an intentional allowed set.**\n\nThe Python profile example specifies `:yang.prelude/max-effects #{}`. Under line 296’s subset rule, this rejects a runtime using cell effects, including `cell/new`.\n\nUse the documented absent/unbounded form for this example, or an explicit sufficient ceiling. This is an example defect; the revised ceiling architecture itself is sound.\n\n**4. Owner-decision framing: D1–D7**\n\n| Decision | Assessment |\n|---|---|\n| **D1 — Local mutation** | Concur with task-local cells and a separately named single-assignment variant. This fits current lowering and `nonlocal` requirements. |\n| **D2 — `yrt.*` naming** | Concur. Linker names and implementation namespaces are clearly distinguished and align with the related designs. |\n| **D3 — Python float tags** | Concur with retaining tags during P1. Representation migration should remain separate from runtime extraction. |\n| **D4 — Batched activation** | Conditional concurrence remains appropriate: validate each extraction independently, then coordinate activation and refresh. Golden changes must follow semantic evidence. |\n| **D5 — Curated projections** | Concur. Explicit aliases, lexical precedence, exact export checks, and reserved-door bans provide a bounded extension surface. |\n| **D6 — Standalone `clojure.core`** | Concur with the constrained direction and unchanged default Clojure bundling. Implementation readiness remains conditional on F4–F5 and the callable-boundary corrections. |\n| **D7 — Two exponentiation orders** | Concur with retaining both algorithms for P1. Unification remains a separate owner-approved semantic change with an oracle-departure record. |\n\nThese remain **owner decisions**, not approvals implied by “Architect concurred.” The revised framing is appropriate; the remaining work is to reconcile the concrete contracts and their acceptance tests.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yang.antlr.md | sed -n '269,329p;389,446p;3640,3699p;4315,4459p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yang.antlr.md | sed -n '269,329p;389,446p;3640,3699p;4315,4459p'\"","aggregated_output":"   269\t## 3. Language frontend SPI\n   270\t\n   271\t### 3.1 Portable declarations versus installed implementations\n   272\t\n   273\tA frontend consists of four independently versioned parts:\n   274\t\n   275\t1. **Grammar package:** lexer/parser grammars, imports, token vocabulary,\n   276\t   entry rules, and required parsing helpers.\n   277\t2. **Syntax export profile:** how the generated parser's CST becomes\n   278\t   portable data.\n   279\t3. **Semantic frontend:** scope analysis, validation, desugaring, and\n   280\t   Universal AST lowering.\n   281\t4. **Runtime profile:** prelude code, value representations, calling\n   282\t   convention, primitive requirements, and permitted effects.\n   283\t\n   284\tA proposed portable manifest might contain:\n   285\t\n   286\t```clojure\n   287\t{:yang.frontend/id :example.ruby/frontend\n   288\t :yang.frontend/spi 1\n   289\t :yang.frontend/revision \"<immutable-package-revision>\"\n   290\t :yang.frontend/language :example.ruby/language\n   291\t\n   292\t :yang.frontend/grammar\n   293\t {:yang.grammar/package \"<content-address>\"\n   294\t  :yang.grammar/lexer \"RubyLexer\"\n   295\t  :yang.grammar/parser \"RubyParser\"\n   296\t  :yang.grammar/entries\n   297\t  {:module \"program\"\n   298\t   :expression \"expression\"}\n   299\t  :yang.grammar/export-profile :yang.cst/v1}\n   300\t\n   301\t :yang.frontend/options-schema \"<content-address>\"\n   302\t :yang.frontend/lowering-profile \"<content-address>\"\n   303\t :yang.frontend/runtime-profile \"<content-address>\"\n   304\t :yang.frontend/support-profile \"<content-address>\"}\n   305\t```\n   306\t\n   307\tThese names are proposed SPI vocabulary, not existing repository APIs.\n   308\tThe strings identify grammar declarations; they are not classes to\n   309\tinstantiate from untrusted input.\n   310\t\n   311\tHost installation separately binds an admitted package revision to:\n   312\t\n   313\t- Generated JVM classes, JavaScript modules, or Dart libraries.\n   314\t- The corresponding ANTLR runtime.\n   315\t- Any target-specific lexer/parser helper implementation.\n   316\t- A portable lowering implementation, or an explicitly selected lowering\n   317\t  service.\n   318\t- Supplied request, response, control, and diagnostic streams.\n   319\t\n   320\tPortable manifests contain no constructors, function objects,\n   321\tclassloaders, callbacks, or live handles.\n   322\t\n   323\t### 3.2 Protocol shape\n   324\t\n   325\tThe contract a frontend satisfies, written as Clojure pseudocode. Every\n   326\tname here is proposed vocabulary that organizes the four parts of 3.1 and\n   327\tthe lowering step of 3.5; none of it exists in the repository today.\n   328\t\n   329\t```clojure\n   389\t### 3.4 Support profiles\n   390\t\n   391\tEach frontend publishes separate claims for:\n   392\t\n   393\t- Syntax recognition by language edition and entry rule.\n   394\t- Semantic analysis.\n   395\t- Executable lowering.\n   396\t- Standard-library compatibility.\n   397\t- Host and effect requirements.\n   398\t- REPL completeness detection.\n   399\t- Optional incremental parsing.\n   400\t\n   401\tFeatures can be declared supported, intentionally restricted, or\n   402\tunsupported, with test identifiers attached to supported claims.\n   403\t\n   404\tA grammar may recognize generators, reflection, or ownership syntax\n   405\tbefore Yang can execute them. Such constructs must produce an\n   406\tunsupported-feature diagnostic at the semantic gate, never a\n   407\tsuperficially valid AST with altered meaning.\n   408\t\n   409\t### 3.5 Lowering contract\n   410\t\n   411\tThe frontend consumes a sealed, validated CST packet and an explicit\n   412\tcompilation environment. Its logical transition is:\n   413\t\n   414\t```text\n   415\tstep(state, observed-input, budget)\n   416\t  -> successor-state\n   417\t   + staged-output\n   418\t   + status\n   419\t```\n   420\t\n   421\tState includes the work stack, scope environment, module facts,\n   422\tdeterministic name supply, intermediate values, and pending outputs.\n   423\t\n   424\tLowering can use:\n   425\t\n   426\t- Declarative patterns over rule names and child roles.\n   427\t- An explicit stack-based tree walker.\n   428\t- Pure visitor functions over normalized data.\n   429\t\n   430\tGenerated ANTLR visitors may help **export syntax inside the host\n   431\tboundary**. They must not invoke portable lowering with\n   432\t`ParserRuleContext` objects.\n   433\t\n   434\tUnknown or unhandled CST constructs are errors. A default visitor that\n   435\tsilently drops children is not acceptable compiler behavior.\n   436\t\n   437\tIf a frontend separates analysis and lowering into independently driven\n   438\tstages, it introduces a stream boundary between them. If they share one\n   439\tinterpreter, their intermediate representation remains private,\n   440\tregenerable state.\n   441\t\n   442\t### 3.6 Adding a language without core changes\n   443\t\n   444\tA third-party author must be able to:\n   445\t\n   446\t1. Package a grammar and audited dependencies.\n  3640\tCross-language passage must not allocate a replacement function cell.\n  3641\tReturned functions retain their original identity, captures, defaults,\n  3642\tbound receiver, properties, and static state. A receiving frontend's\n  3643\tcallable test follows this common eligibility rule rather than rejecting\n  3644\tforeign native tags. Foreign attribute access and type reflection remain\n  3645\tprofile-owned; callability grants no general right to interpret another\n  3646\truntime's cell layout.\n  3647\t\n  3648\tCompiler-internal lambdas and host functions do not acquire this\n  3649\tconvention merely by being applicable in Yin. A source import exposing\n  3650\tone must emit an entry from its declared signature and effect profile.\n  3651\tIt must not guess an opaque closure's signature or bypass host-effect\n  3652\tand custody rules. Internal helper calls need not construct records.\n  3653\t\n  3654\t#### 8.10.3 Invocation record\n  3655\t\n  3656\tThe invocation is an ephemeral in-machine value. These keys are proposed\n  3657\tvocabulary, not existing repository APIs:\n  3658\t\n  3659\t```clojure\n  3660\t{:yang.call/callee     f\n  3661\t :yang.call/positional [v0 v1 ...]\n  3662\t :yang.call/named      [[\"name\" value] ...]\n  3663\t :yang.call/raise      raise-exit\n  3664\t ;; Optional: presence means a receiver was supplied, even when nil.\n  3665\t :yang.call/receiver   receiver\n  3666\t ;; Optional: pinned argument-binding extension, for example locations.\n  3667\t :yang.call/binding-profile \"<content-address>\"}\n  3668\t```\n  3669\t\n  3670\tThe four required fields mean:\n  3671\t\n  3672\t- `:yang.call/callee`: the original resolved callable, including its\n  3673\t  identity. The invocation helper supplies it; an extracted entry must\n  3674\t  not be applied to another object's substituted state.\n  3675\t- `:yang.call/positional`: a dense ordered vector of evaluated values.\n  3676\t  Its length records presence; no missing-argument sentinel is inserted.\n  3677\t- `:yang.call/named`: ordered name/value pairs, not a map. Names are\n  3678\t  plain text without caller-side case folding or normalization.\n  3679\t  Duplicate names remain visible until the callee's binder decides them.\n  3680\t- `:yang.call/raise`: this activation's caller-supplied, non-returning\n  3681\t  guest exit, accepting the condition in section 8.10.7. It closes over\n  3682\t  the caller's continuation and exception machinery, never a host\n  3683\t  callback or hidden global handler slot.\n  3684\t\n  3685\tAn absent receiver differs from one whose value is `nil`, `:py/None`,\n  3686\t`:php/null`, `:js/null`, or `:js/undefined`. Empty argument vectors are\n  3687\trequired rather than represented by absent keys. Presence must not be\n  3688\tinferred from truthiness.\n  3689\t\n  3690\tThe optional binding profile pins additional argument semantics. It\n  3691\tdoes not name the caller's language or select a language-pair bridge.\n  3692\tAbsent that extension, arguments are by-value runtime values as in\n  3693\tsection 8.10.6. The callee entry compares the supplied profile address\n  3694\twith its declared accepted/required binding profiles before acquiring\n  3695\treferences or entering the body. An unsupported required profile or a\n  3696\tmissing required extension fails there. The pinned profile defines the\n  3697\tshape and validation of any extra invocation fields it requires; values\n  3698\tin the base vectors alone do not establish a location protocol.\n  3699\t\n  4315\t## 9. Standard library and FFI architecture\n  4316\t\n  4317\tEvery language arrives with a standard library, and most of what a\n  4318\tprogram observes as \"the language\" is that library. This section places\n  4319\teach part of a language's standard library and host interface in one of\n  4320\tfour layers. The placement follows the semantic placement rule of\n  4321\tsection 8.2 and the host-boundary rule of `datom.world.md`: portable\n  4322\tcode appends effects, host interpreters perform them and append outcomes,\n  4323\tand no host function is ever called from portable code.\n  4324\t\n  4325\t### 9.1 The four-layer model\n  4326\t\n  4327\t```text\n  4328\t+-------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------+\n  4329\t| Layer | Contents                    | How it reaches the VM         |\n  4330\t+=======+=============================+===============================+\n  4331\t| 1     | Pure modules: library code  | Compiled by Yang, exactly as  |\n  4332\t|       | written in the language     | user code; no runtime         |\n  4333\t|       | itself                      | special-casing                |\n  4334\t+-------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------+\n  4335\t| 2     | Language intrinsics and     | Universal AST prelude per     |\n  4336\t|       | preludes: the initial       | language, compiled once,      |\n  4337\t|       | environment                 | content-addressed, fetched    |\n  4338\t|       |                             | from the dao.jing DHT         |\n  4339\t+-------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------+\n  4340\t| 3     | Object system desugaring:   | Records, store cells, and     |\n  4341\t|       | classes, prototypes, traits | closures; no object concept   |\n  4342\t|       |                             | enters the VM kernel          |\n  4343\t+-------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------+\n  4344\t| 4     | System I/O and effects:     | Yin VM effect descriptors;    |\n  4345\t|       | files, sockets, timers,     | Local dao.stream.apply FFI;   |\n  4346\t|       | processes, native libraries | host interpreters append      |\n  4347\t|       |                             | outcomes                      |\n  4348\t+-------+-----------------------------+-------------------------------+\n  4349\t```\n  4350\t\n  4351\t### 9.2 Layer 1: pure modules\n  4352\t\n  4353\tSource in the language itself, compiled to Universal AST via Yang\n  4354\tdirectly. A Python `itertools` written in Python, a Java collections\n  4355\tclass written in Java, a Clojure `clojure.core` function written in\n  4356\tClojure: each is an ordinary compilation unit that passes through the\n  4357\tsection 5 ingestion and section 6 pipeline and is admitted like any\n  4358\tother program. There is no runtime special-casing: the evaluator cannot\n  4359\ttell library code from user code, and nothing in Yang core names a\n  4360\tlibrary module.\n  4361\t\n  4362\t### 9.3 Layer 2: language intrinsics and preludes\n  4363\t\n  4364\tA Universal AST prelude per language seeds the initial environment. It\n  4365\tcarries what section 8.2 assigns to portable prelude code: value\n  4366\tencodings, truthiness, equality, arithmetic semantics, argument binding,\n  4367\tcoercions, collections, and language exceptions.\n  4368\t\n  4369\tA prelude is compiled once and content-addressed; its root addresses are\n  4370\tnamed in the frontend's runtime profile (section 3.1, part 4). It is\n  4371\tdistributed via the `dao.jing` DHT, so every host that runs a language\n  4372\tresolves the same prelude by the same address and never rebuilds it. A\n  4373\tcomposition that pins a runtime profile pins its prelude addresses; two\n  4374\tcompositions running different prelude revisions do not interfere.\n  4375\t\n  4376\tPreludes are Universal AST code, so they are inspectable, queryable by\n  4377\t`dao.space.query/q` over their rows, and portable across CLJ, CLJS, and\n  4378\tCLJD without per-host reimplementation.\n  4379\t\n  4380\tThe pure data primitives a prelude needs (length, deletion, removal,\n  4381\tstring operations) live in a `:pure` host module named by the language's\n  4382\truntime profile, not in the standard `vm/primitives` registry, whose\n  4383\tchange would affect every composition (mutable-objects ruling, owner\n  4384\tdecision 3). The module is `yin.vm.data`, module name `data`, landed in\n  4385\t`fe8bce4a`: a composition whose runtime profile names it installs it\n  4386\texplicitly with `register-data-module`. It is a registry addition, not a\n  4387\tgrammar change. Its strings are code-point indexed on every host, and no\n  4388\texport iterates a host map.\n  4389\t\n  4390\tPython's exact integers follow the same pattern with a second module: a\n  4391\tversioned `:pure` integer module that the Python runtime profile\n  4392\trequires and the composition installs explicitly (C3 ruling 5, section\n  4393\t8.5.4). It has landed in C3 slice S1 (`54536317`).\n  4394\t\n  4395\t### 9.4 Layer 3: object system desugaring\n  4396\t\n  4397\tClass, prototype, and trait hierarchies are desugared to records, store\n  4398\tcells, and closures:\n  4399\t\n  4400\t- A class becomes a runtime descriptor record plus field layout and\n  4401\t  method code (section 8.4).\n  4402\t- A prototype chain becomes property maps in store cells linked by\n  4403\t  logical identity (section 8.7).\n  4404\t- A trait or interface becomes a dispatch contract the prelude resolves\n  4405\t  at call time.\n  4406\t\n  4407\tNo object system concept crosses into the VM kernel. The canonical\n  4408\tgrammar of `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` section 2.3 has no class, method,\n  4409\tprototype, or trait tag, and this document adds none. Dispatch, method\n  4410\tresolution, and inheritance are prelude code (layer 2) operating on data\n  4411\tthat desugaring produced.\n  4412\t\n  4413\t\"Store cells\" here are `cell` module refs over the task heap (section\n  4414\t8.1). A mutable object, including a class, is one cell holding one\n  4415\tpersistent value, and a mutable collection is likewise one cell; section\n  4416\t8.11 gives the representation, identity, and aliasing rules.\n  4417\t\n  4418\t### 9.5 Layer 4: system I/O and effects\n  4419\t\n  4420\tSystem I/O is expressed as Yin VM effect descriptors. Portable code\n  4421\tappends a descriptor naming the operation, its arguments, and a\n  4422\tcorrelation identity; a host interpreter consumes that stream, performs\n  4423\tthe operation, and appends the outcome; portable code reads the outcome.\n  4424\tA host with no implementation for an effect emits a qualified\n  4425\tunsupported result, which is a correct outcome.\n  4426\t\n  4427\tFFI calls use `dao.stream.apply` as the VM's local FFI bridge. The call\n  4428\tis a request record on a stream, the result is a response record, and\n  4429\tcorrelation is by identity in the data. The apply wire envelope is\n  4430\tretired from the network path; remote service traffic uses request and\n  4431\tanswer streams over `dao.stream.remote` reflections. No direct host\n  4432\tfunction calls from portable code. Browser APIs, Node APIs, JVM\n  4433\tlibraries, and Dart platform channels are effect packages declared in\n  4434\ta frontend's runtime profile, not properties of any grammar.\n  4435\t\n  4436\tCapabilities are declared: the runtime profile lists the effects a\n  4437\tlanguage's standard library may request, and a composition grants or\n  4438\twithholds them. Inside the VM, effects are an unforgeable host type and\n  4439\tthe engine checks an effect raised by a primitive against the callee's\n  4440\tdeclared effect set (section 8.1, mob D4).\n  4441\t\n  4442\t### 9.6 Module distribution\n  4443\t\n  4444\tStandard-library modules, preludes, and any compiled package are\n  4445\tcontent-addressed packages in the `dao.jing` DHT. A package is a manifest\n  4446\tnaming the root addresses it exports plus the rows those roots reach;\n  4447\tequal content is equal address across hosts. Module resolution consumes\n  4448\trequests and emits pinned module artifacts (section 11); a dependency\n  4449\tedge names a content address, never a search path or a network location.\n  4450\t\n  4451\t---\n  4452\t\n  4453\t## 10. Diagnostics and error reporting\n  4454\t\n  4455\tANTLR's default behavior combines error reporting and recovery. Console\n  4456\treporting is supplied by a listener, while recovery may insert/delete\n  4457\ttokens, continue, or raise recognition errors. The integration must\n  4458\tcontrol both mechanisms.\n  4459\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
