{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_108","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-11-20 17:42:53 GMT  \nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-21 00:42:53 ICT\n\n## 1. Verdict: `REVISE`\n\n**Five r2 findings are `CONFIRMED-FIXED`. F1, F3, and F4 remain `OPEN`.** The value-returning exception boundary, cell-backed closure construction, vector frame access, boxed float status interface, and active effect-ceiling example each resolve the specific defect reported in r2.\n\nThe design still permits an integer limit result to become a host failure, specifies a float floor-division algorithm that returns the wrong integer for ordinary finite inputs, and leaves the path from a published library export to an invocable task-local callable incomplete. These affect P1 and P3 acceptance.\n\nSession ID: `01a1fa0a-94cf-7df3-acd0-16353342ed04`. This was a read-only design and source review. I checked the floor-division and power witnesses with in-memory Python arithmetic. No files were edited. Repository suites and four-host acceptance gates were not run.\n\n## 2. r2 finding-by-finding disposition\n\n| ID | r2 Finding | Status | Evidence / Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| F1 | Portable numeric classification, guarded dispatch, and collection equality | **OPEN** | The classifier now recognizes boxed CLJS floats. Negative integer powers retain an exact exponent magnitude, and `%` guards the `fmod-pos` domain. The finite `//` algorithm still misrounds. Float exponent conversion can pass `::bit-limit` to `integer/compare-float`. Recursive equality also leaves key insertion and lookup dependent on host equality. [Numeric dispatch](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:528) · [Collection equality](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:554) |\n| F2 | Function entry loses normal results and misclassifies binding errors | **CONFIRMED-FIXED** | `try-value` surrounds both binding and execution. A normal body result such as `20` is returned verbatim. Named-argument and positional-arity errors enter the callee’s exception path and cross under `:yang.call/thrown`. Retained protocol conditions have a separate forwarding path. The additional protocol-record defect below occurs before binding. [Revised entry](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:604) · [Exception contract](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:3843) |\n| F3 | Source-level callable admission before structural invocation | **OPEN** | The specified sequence of checks is sound for the profile’s stated cell-backed interop surface, including rejection of unauthenticated maps. The supplied guest definition uses `and`, which the designated prelude emitter lowers as an ordinary application. Also, standalone `fn?` permits a raw-callable alternative that its dynamic caller rejects. [Admission predicate](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:591) · [Standalone predicate](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:628) |\n| F4 | Portable standalone `clojure.core` numerics and equality | **OPEN** | The `inc`, `dec`, and `int?` rewrites address the named carrier problem. `rem` indexes the kernel result before checking its status. Retained exports still expose host arithmetic, formatting, and collection behavior. [Portable subset](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:620) · [Pair-or-reason kernel](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc:285) |\n| F5 | Dynamic closure construction and internal higher-order calls | **CONFIRMED-FIXED** | The construction rule explicitly covers closures created during `comp` and `partial`. It preserves the returned cell’s identity. Static sibling calls use internal lambdas, while dynamic callback calls use the public invocation convention. The separate linker projection and provenance-ownership issues below concern how that contract is reached. [Construction and dispatch](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:633) |\n| F6 | Capture-safe escape-frame access in bundled Clojure | **CONFIRMED-FIXED** | Every frame slot is read through qualified `data/nth`. A source definition of `get` can no longer redirect a vector read into the map-only fallback. The P1 gate exercises nested handlers and finalizers under that shadowing case. [Frame access](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:149) · [Existing fallback](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2484) |\n| F7 | Raw doubles passed through guest policy callbacks | **CONFIRMED-FIXED** | The exceptional public interfaces take boxed inputs and return a boxed value, exact integer, or status keyword. Language policy runs after the raw arithmetic frame exits. P1 still needs the corrected underflow witness below. [Isolation rule](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:185) · [Result contracts](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:205) |\n| F8 | Python example rejects itself with an empty effect ceiling | **CONFIRMED-FIXED** | Both active examples now use `nil`, explicitly meaning no extra ceiling. The subset rule remains appropriate for a supplied set. The parenthetical four-effect set is insufficient for Python and should be corrected. [Recipe](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:263) · [Resolved profile](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:283) |\n\n### Recheck of the five findings closed in r2\n\n| r1 finding | r3 confirmation |\n|---|---|\n| **#2 — Frontend-owned Hook ABI and structural lowering** | **Ownership remains fixed.** The frontend owns grammar-specific signatures and structural lowering. Per-unit globals, five-argument function construction, three-argument `try`, and generator-expression refusal remain explicit. The advertised catch syntax and the call to `clojure.core/seq` need the concrete corrections below. [Ownership](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:321) · [Hook ABI](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:569) |\n| **#5 — Generator frames and context migration** | **Confirmed without regression.** Non-`:finally` dispatch remains open to generator frames. Slot 3 counts frames and slot 4 restores activation depth. P4 preserves Python rebasing and PHP’s direct restoration of the saved generator context. [Frame contract](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:143) · [Generator policy](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:225) |\n| **#8 — Integer v4 refusal algebra** | **The shared contract remains fixed.** §4.4 preserves all three returned reasons, operational throws, and PHP-owned demotion. F1 and F4 identify new consumers that violate that contract. [Shared contract](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:214) · [Kernel contract](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc:81) |\n| **#9 — Recipe/profile separation and verified closure** | **The r2 correction remains in place.** The resolved profile pins the owning root and the design requires verified dependency manifests. Admission still needs an explicit source for the installed numeric limits, and descriptor targets must resolve against the selected closure. [Profile and closure](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:266) |\n| **#10 — Roadmap and acceptance gates** | **Phase boundaries remain fixed. A P1 gate has regressed.** P0 and P2 retain byte-parity requirements, P3 preserves default Clojure bundling, and D4 requires independent validation before activation. P1 now lists a successful zero result as its exceptional underflow witness. [Roadmap](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:695) |\n\n## 3. New or remaining findings\n\n| Severity | File:line | Invariant / evidence | Recommended correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **HIGH** | [yang.preludes.md:541](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:541) | For `2.1 // 0.7`, the specified intermediate quotient is `2.9999999999999996`. Flooring gives `2.0`. The floored quotient is `3.0`. The live Python helper corrects the rounded quotient and separately preserves the sign of a zero quotient. The proposed exact-integer floor also loses that sign. [Live divmod calculation](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:1425) | Specify the finite quotient correction and signed-zero branch. Pin `2.1 // 0.7` and `-0.0 // 2.0`. Also state the float/mixed `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, and unary adapter rules, including the `/` zero-divisor policy. §7.2 names those operations but gives detailed float dispatch only for `//`, `%`, `**`, and ordering. |\n| **HIGH** | [yang.preludes.md:549](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:549), [line 624](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:624) | At the admitted 53-bit floor, the finite float exponent `2.0^53` requires a 54-bit integer. `integer/from-float` can return `::bit-limit`, which the proposed expression passes to `integer/compare-float`. Likewise, `quot-rem` returns either a pair or a reason, and `rem` applies `data/nth` first. Both routes can turn a guest resource refusal into a host wrong-type failure. [Float conversion](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc:454) · [Pair contract](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc:178) | Unwrap boxed float inputs at the leaf, classify the conversion result before comparison, and translate the status after leaving the raw frame. Check `quot-rem` before indexing. Apply the same rule to `integer/neg` on externally supplied exact operands. Test at the minimum admitted limit. |\n| **HIGH** | [yang.preludes.md:561](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:561), [line 632](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:632) | Recursive comparison cannot recover keys or elements already collapsed by host collection construction. Normalizing a top-level `-0.0` misses a vector key containing it. Projected `assoc`, `get`, `conj`, and retained `contains?` also bypass the stated normalization. Jing explicitly refuses certain equal-value, different-kind key collisions. [Data-module key contract](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc:34) · [Jing collection contract](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:173) | Define one portable key/element domain for construction, update, lookup, and membership, including nested keys, NaNs, and equal-valued integer/float keys. A v1 restriction with an explicit guest refusal is sufficient. Test literal construction followed by projected update and lookup on every host. |\n| **HIGH** | [yang.preludes.md:594](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:594), [line 628](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:628) | The common emitter recognizes `if`, `let`, and a small set of other forms. It emits `and` as an application, and `and` has no standard VM primitive binding. The displayed predicate therefore cannot provide the required short circuit as written. Separately, `data/callable?` includes host functions and continuations. The optional `fn?` branch can report true for a value the standalone higher-order dispatcher refuses. [Emitter](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/core/uast.cljc:82) · [VM primitives](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:396) · [Applicability predicate](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc:456) | Emit nested `if` nodes or explicitly expand short-circuit syntax before emission. Use the same source admission predicate in `fn?`, ordinary calls, and higher-order calls. Keep raw implementation closures on the private direct-call path. |\n| **HIGH** | [yang.preludes.md:605](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:605) | `yang.call/valid-invocation?` checks record shape and field presence. It does not establish an applicable raise exit, check callee identity, or reject an unsupported `:yang.call/binding-profile`. A well-shaped extended record can reach the body with the extension silently ignored. Conversely, a missing or invalid exit cannot carry `:yang.call/invalid-invocation`. [Shared validator](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc:76) · [Extension and exit obligations](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:3690) · [Python entry precedent](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:906) | Establish the exit first. Treat an unusable exit as an operational wiring failure. Check owner identity and supported binding profiles before binding, transferring `:yang.call/invalid-invocation` or `:yang.call/unsupported-binding` through the valid exit. Keep ordinary parameter errors inside `try-value`. |\n| **HIGH** | [yang.preludes.md:580](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:580), [line 634](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:634) | Public library functions are task-local cells, yet the external `clj.py/for-each` adapter directly applies `(clojure.core/seq iterable)`. The direct sibling-lambda rule applies only inside `clojure.core`. There is also no specified lookup that turns an immutable linker export into its later `init!`-allocated source cell. The linker snapshots exports at child halt, resolves imports from lowered bindings, and refuses to lift task-heap cells. [Export snapshot](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1055) · [Cell-lift refusal](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:915) · [Import resolution](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:40) | Specify a linkable accessor or factory and an idempotent per-task export table. Resolve a projected source name to its stable task-local cell. Invoke that cell through the common convention, or give trusted adapters a separately named, pinned implementation entry. Gate two imports in one task for identity and two tasks for isolation. |\n| **HIGH** | [yang.preludes.md:614](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:614), [line 637](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:637) | The private provenance bridge is restricted to trusted Layer 2b and described as `module-uast` only. Layer 2c `clojure.core` is nevertheless assigned the same entry and caller bridges. Bundled execution also owes the same retained-condition behavior. In addition, `clj.py` uses `clj/rt-ctx` despite the per-profile handler isolation rule. [Ownership invariants](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:68) · [Bundled parity law](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:303) | Assign the bridge and its context cell to a named trusted runtime. Specify how library entries and both emitters use it without exposing the provenance doors. If `clj.py` intentionally shares Clojure’s exception domain, record that decision and test nested `clj` → `clj.py` → `clj` cleanup. |\n| **HIGH** | [yang.preludes.md:621](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:621) | The retained “non-numeric” surface still includes `fresh-symbol`, which increments the caller’s `:counter` with bare `+`. Projected `str` delegates to host `str`, so boxed `1.0` can render as `\"1\"` on JS and `\"1.0\"` on the JVM. `keys` and `vals` expose host map iteration order as an ordered sequence. [Counter and string definitions](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2689) · [JS carrier formatting](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/host/number.cljc:76) · [Keys and values](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2861) | Audit the retained exports by observable operation. Use the integer kernel for caller-owned counters, specify portable string rendering and map traversal, or narrow the exported domains. Include these exports in the four-host gate. |\n| **HIGH** | [yang.preludes.md:379](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:379), [line 466](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:466) | `:raw-or-call` carries an executable `:overflow-op`. The exact-export and reserved-door checks enumerate `:hooks` and `:ambient-projections`, leaving this literal-strategy target outside the stated checks. Finding an export somewhere in `artifact-store` also does not establish that the selected runtime closure authorizes it for that position. | Validate every descriptor field that can emit a call against the selected, address-verified closure and its frontend-authorized role. Apply qualification, arity, and reserved-door rules to literal strategies as well as hooks and projections. |\n| **MEDIUM** | [yang.preludes.md:302](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:302) | The proposed validator promises to check numeric floors from the registry. Host registration publishes export profiles and functions. `integer/max-bits` is absent from the export inventory, and the existing Python registrar checks the explicit limits map before constructing the registry. [Host registration](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:249) · [Integer exports](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc:592) · [Python registration](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:4163) | Pass the trusted composition limits into admission or retain verifiable limit declarations at registration. Distinguish the root’s direct `:requires` pins from the transitively verified closure so a nested dependency need not be declared as a direct edge. |\n| **MEDIUM** | [yang.preludes.md:698](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:698), [line 182](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:182) | `2.0 ** -2000` returns boxed `+0.0` on the existing split-reciprocal path. The exceptional status applies when the *positive power in the denominator* rounds to zero. The dependency list also requires `data/float64?` for `yrt.float`, while §7.2 makes its installation optional in P1. [Existing negative-power algorithm](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:1530) · [Prerequisite schedule](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:524) | Keep `2.0 ** -2000` as a normal-zero witness. Use `0.5 ** -2000` for the underflow status and outer overflow policy, and keep `2.0 ** -1074` for the nonzero subnormal path. Put the new predicate in the first phase that actually requires it. |\n| **MEDIUM** | [yang.preludes.md:367](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:367), [line 580](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:580) | Python’s grammar permits `as name` only after an exception type. The advertised `except as e:` is unparseable. Eager comprehensions are also advertised over immutable collections, while the current lowering mutates an accumulator and ignores each step result. A traversal hook alone leaves the persistent result undefined. [Grammar](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/antlr/python3/Python3Parser.g4:313) · [Comprehension emission](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:2302) | Advertise bare `except:` for this profile’s catch-all form. Specify how list, set, and dict comprehensions thread or store each updated accumulator, and gate a nonempty result for each kind. |\n| **LOW** | [yang.preludes.md:283](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:283) | The active `nil` ceiling works. The adjacent four-effect example omits at least `:cell/new-provenance` and `:cell/provenance`, both used by the Python runtime. [Python host dependencies](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:204) · [Declared effects](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:446) | Remove the illustrative set or derive a sufficient example from the verified Python closure. |\n\n## 4. Owner-decision framing\n\n| Decision | r3 confirmation |\n|---|---|\n| **D1 — Local mutation** | Concur with **A**. Task-local lexical cells support reassignment and `nonlocal`. Keep single-assignment as a separately identified restricted profile. |\n| **D2 — Module prefix** | Concur with **A**. `yrt.*` linker names and `yang.rt.*` implementation namespaces have distinct, clear roles. |\n| **D3 — Python float tags** | Concur with **A**. Retain `{:py/float ...}` during P1 to preserve Python’s observable representation across extraction. |\n| **D4 — Batched activation** | **Conditional concurrence with A.** Validate each extraction independently, including corrected numeric and exceptional-path witnesses, before the coordinated activation and golden refresh. |\n| **D5 — Identifier mapping** | Concur with **A**. Curated aliases and lexical precedence fit the SPI. Apply the same authority checks to every descriptor field that emits an executable reference. |\n| **D6 — Standalone `clojure.core`** | **Conditional concurrence with constrained A.** Keep default Clojure publication intact during P3. Resolve the export-to-task-cell path, trusted bridge ownership, and portable subset before treating the new module as implementation-ready. |\n| **D7 — Exponentiation rounding** | Concur with **A** for P1. Preserve both evaluation orders and their distinct `1.1 ** 6` bit patterns. Align §10’s export names with the `-raw` names in §4.3. A future unification remains an explicit owner decision. |\n\nD1–D7 remain open for owner disposition. Architectural concurrence records a recommendation. The `REVISE` verdict concerns the concrete contracts and acceptance gates above."}}
